L700: **animal studies (4000 character limit).** [NS 1.1](http://www.neaf.gov.au /referenceMaterial/RefTopicView.aspx?TOC=1&reference_id=1408)
L701: The nutritional supplement industry is recommending DAA as a viable product to raise basal levels of
L702: testosterone. Recent research indicates that daily supplementation (3g per day) of d-aspartic acid (DAA) has
L703: the potential to increase resting levels of testosterone in untrained male participants (1). Research has
L704: demonstrated that raising levels of testosterone from exogenous administration of testosterone has a
L705: significant effect on adaptation from a training stimulus. This has not been studied with the administration of
L706: DAA. Anabolic hormones that can contribute to hypertrophy or strength gains include testosterone, insulin
L707: like growth factor 1 (IGF-1) and human growth hormone (GH). IGF-1 can increase due to resistance training
L708: (2, 3), but research has also shown non-significant changes (4-6). Basal levels of growth hormone tend not
L709: to change from resistance training (2, 6-8). This may be attributed to the large variety in isoforms produced
L710: in the body and the limited availability to detect them with current technology. Of all the hormones in the
L711: body testosterone is considered to be one of the most powerful androgenic-anabolic hormones secreted
L712: naturally by the body (9).
L713: Evidence for the hypertrophic effects of exogenously increasing anabolic hormones has existed anecdotally
L714: for some time and research indicates these hypertrophic effects are true in normal men (10). In elderly
L715: people testosterone enanthate has been shown to improve fasting net protein balance (11), via a significant
L716: decrease in muscle protein breakdown. Testosterone has also been associated with fibre hypertrophy or
L717: more specifically the addition of myonuclei to the muscle type II fibers (12). In healthy men when
L718: testosterone concentrations were suppressed over 10 weeks, there was a reduction in an index of whole
L719: body protein synthesis (-13%), and all subjects had a decrease in fat-free mass (13).
L720: Testosterone is also believed to stimulate the proliferation and differentiation of satellite cells (SC) (14). This
L721: has been demonstrated in young (15) and old (16) people. Satellite cells are an important mechanism of
L722: hypertrophy as they are activated by myotrauma caused by resistance training. Then the SC proliferate,
L723: move to the damaged region, where they fuse to the damaged myofiber and are eventually integrated
L724: resulting in hypertrophy (17).
L725: It is well known that resistance exercise training leads to hypertrophy of skeletal muscle. This growth is a
L726: result of an increase in cross sectional area (CSA) and total volume of the muscle. Increased force output is
L727: a product of increased CSA in the muscle and neural adaptation (18, 19). The interaction of testosterone
L728: with ARs is not limited to muscle cells, as ARs can also be found on motor neurons. Data on gerbil studies
L729: indicates that testosterone interacting with these AR may maintain the relative size of the motor neurons (14)
L730: throughout normal life. When coupled with a resistance training program, the effect on strength and
L731: hypertrophy is enhanced (10). Testosterone administration has also been positively correlated with leg press
L732: strength, leg power and IGF levels (20). The increase in testosterone from exogenous administration of
L733: 600mg testosterone enanthate ranges from 600%-700%, which is considered supraphysiological levels (10).
L734: This is significantly greater than an expected increase from DAA.
L735: Study 1 will measure testosterone levels before and after 12 days of DAA supplemented daily. Study 2 will
L736: Commercial-in-Confidence
L737: Page 12 of 32
L738: investigate the effects of the elevation of testosterone and any subsequent effects on training outcomes
L739: following a resistance exercise program. Training outcomes measured will be changes in strength
L740: hypertrophy and adaptation of the peripheral somatic nervous system.
L741: Consequently, this thesis will be based upon novel research that strives to study the effectiveness of DAA
L742: both for its potential to raise testosterone levels and the effect on training outcomes in a resistance trained
L743: population.
L744: **5.2.2 State the aims of the research and the research question and/or hypotheses, where appropriate.**
L745: Research Aim
L746: To measure the effect that d-aspartic supplementation has on basal testosterone levels in advanced
L747: trainers.
L748: Primary Objectives
L749: 1. To measure testosterone levels changes.
L750: 2. To measure the change in 1RM strength
L751: 3. To measure the change in muscle CSA
L752: 4. To measure adaptation of the peripheral somatic nervous system
L753: 5. To explore the correlation of strength to other outcome measures.
L754: 4.3 Hypothesis
L755: H1: Supplementation of 6g of DAA will elevate basal testosterone levels more than 3g of DAA as compared
L756: to the placebo.
L757: H2: The greatest elevation in testosterone will result in greater gains in 1RM strength, which will be
L758: explained by hypertrophy of the CSA of the quadriceps muscle and adaptation of the peripheral somatic
L759: nervous system.
L760: **5.2.3 Has this project been undertaken previously?** No
L761: **5.3. Benefits/Risks**
L762: **5.3.0 Does the research involve a practice or intervention which is an alternative to a**
L763: **standard practice or intervention?**
L764: **5.3.2 What expected benefits (if any) will this research have for the wider community?**
L765: No
L766: This research will provide information to the various people using d-aspartic acid (DAA) on the effectiveness
L767: of DAA to raise testosterone levels, along with informing these people of the extent to which this will help
L768: with their training goals. The proposed research will help clarify the effectiveness of DAA as a supplement
L769: for improved gains in strength as moderated by elevated levels of testosterone. Furthermore the current
L770: research will potentially open pathways for future research to explore the use of this supplement in other
L771: cohorts (elderly).
L772: **5.3.3 What expected benefits (if any) will this research have for participants?** [NS 2.1](http://www.neaf.gov.au /referenceMaterial/RefTopicView.aspx?TOC=1&reference_id=1320)
L773: All participants will receive expert advice in exercise and training. All participants should expect to receive
L774: improvements in physical strength and hypertrophy regardless of being assigned to placebo or experimental
L775: groups. Experimental groups will be given d-aspartic acid supplement at no cost for the duration of the
L776: study. All groups will receive post-workout nutrition.
L777: **5.3.4 Are there any risks to participants as a result of participation in this research**
L778: **project?** [NS 2.1](http://www.neaf.gov.au /referenceMaterial/RefTopicView.aspx?TOC=1&reference_id=1320)
L779: Yes
L780: **5.3.5 Explain how the likely benefit of the research justifies the risks of harm or discomfort to participants.**
L781: [NS 1.6](http://www.neaf.gov.au /referenceMaterial/RefTopicView.aspx?TOC=1&reference_id=1410)
L782: The resistance exercise intervention has been designed and will be supervised by a Geoffrey Melville
L783: (qualifications noted in section 2).Testing procedures are routine and standardised and include physical and
L784: screening assessments.
L785: These assessments will be delivered by Geoffrey Melville and have been used across a range of cohorts.
L786: The current project involves healthy men who are at an advanced training level. Therefore, for the purposes
L787: of ethical review, this study should be considered high risk. In the case of an emergency or adverse events
L788: related to the resistance training program, emergency services (i.e. ambulance) will be notified and/or the
L789: participant will be referred to a qualified professional for follow up, as indicated. Additional risks of this study
L790: involve a small amount of discomfort experienced during blood collection, and some discomfort during
L791: neural adaptation testing methodologies.
L792: Commercial-in-Confidence
L793: Page 13 of 32
L794: The subjects will be informed of all steps of all the parts of the protocol as it progress’s to ensure that the
L795: stress and anxiety of being a participant in this study is minimised.
L796: As with any product consumed or brought into contact with humans, there is a risk of developing an allergic
L797: reaction. During the study all participants will be asked to consume a drink of maltodextrin (sugar) and whey
L798: protein isolate (dairy protein) for postworkout nutrition. Therefore anyone with a known allergy to these
L799: products is instantly excluded from the study. During the study participants assigned to the experimental
L800: groups will be consuming the supplement d-aspartic acid. If you have a known allergy to this product or
L801: other amino acids you will also be excluded from the study.
L802: Human research into the product d-aspartic acid is still in early stages and thus all of the potential risks
L803: associated with consumption are unknown. Known side-effects of illegal testosterone supplementation (e.g.
L804: anabolic steroids) include increased estradiol, reduced HDL cholesterol (good cholesterol) acne, hair loss,
L805: gynecomastia, liver damage, mood changes and atrophy of the testicles. While it is thought that d-aspartic
L806: acid is likely to raise basal testosterone, current evidence from humans does not suggest the elevation will
L807: be to a level associated with reported side-effects of anabolic steroid use. Anecdotal reports of side-effects
L808: associated with d-aspartic acid use must be weighed against the likelihood people are also using a variety of
L809: other substances that may induce these side-effects. We do not expect any side-effects to be experienced
L810: (e.g. reduced HDL cholesterol, increased acne or increased estradiol). However, we will be monitoring
L811: levels of estradiol, HDL cholesterol and unwanted physical changes. Furthermore, if participants notice or
L812: experience any physical changes that they are not used to, or do not expect, they are to contact Geoffrey
L813: Melville or any of the research team as soon as possible.
L814: Exogenous administration of testosterone to healthy males can decrease levels of HDL cholesterol. While it
L815: is hard to predict the potential health risks, it has been speculated that if this reduction in HDL cholesterol
L816: was maintained over a period of years the risk of CAD in some men might increase by about 20%. For
L817: study 2, HDL cholesterol will be analysed and monitored as a precautionary measure at baseline, 3 months
L818: and 6 months.
L819: Participants will be reminded that they are free to withdraw at any point.
L820: **5.3.8 Are there any other risks involved in this research? eg. to the research team, the**
L821: **organisation, others**
L822: **5.3.8... What are these risks?**
L823: Yes
L824: The monitoring of basal testosterone and HDL cholesterol can only be achieved via obtaining whole blood.
L825: Measuring blood in this manner (capillary and/or venous) is also essential for determining a) the magnitude
L826: of hormonal alteration and b) the relationships to be determined between change in testosterone and
L827: hypertrophy, strength and neural adaptation.
L828: The collection of blood involves the use of needles and lancets. All sharps carry a risk of injury as a sharp,
L829: by definition, is anything that can cut or pierce your skin. Potentially there could also be blood borne viruses
L830: in the blood sample.
L831: **5.3.8... Explain how these risks will be negated/minimised/managed.**
L832: However, the risk of exposure to such viruses and needle-stick injury will be minimised by following the SOP
L833: for capillary and venous blood sampling (see SOPs attached) and appropriate safety measures (e.g.
L834: wearing of exam gloves, glasses and lab coat).
L835: Once blood is obtained in vacutainer it will be centrifuged to obtain serum. The serum will be aliquoted into
L836: epindorf tubes and stored in -80oC freezer until all samples are acquired. Vacutainer method provides less
L837: chance of blood spilling, reducing the risk of exposure.
L838: **5.3.8... Explain how these risks will be monitored.**
L839: Compliance with this policy will be monitored through the annual audit process in School of Science and
L840: Health (SSH) and overseen by the Health & Safety Officer. It will be the day to day responsibility of all SSH
L841: staff members to monitor that the requirements of this procedure are being adhered too, and that
L842: appropriate risk control measures are in place. SSH staff members are responsible for ensuring those
L843: affected have followed the reporting and management procedures highlighted in the following policy:
L844: SOP_0018 Handling Accidental Inoculation Incidents.
L845: **5.3.8... Explain how any harm to participants, resulting from these risks,** **will be reported.**
L846: Immediate report to the injury to the senior technician, Technical Manager and Health & Safety Office
L847: (OH&S);
L848: Complete the University’s Incident Report Form and follow the OH&S policy and procedures for Accident,
L849: Injury, Incident, Hazard Reporting and Investigation.
L850: Commercial-in-Confidence
